      "type": "thinking",
      "thinking": "Let me analyze this fragment of the OIML certificate.\n\nKey info:\n- Certificate number: R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01, revision 6\n- Scheme: A\n- Project number: 3489487\n- Page total: 11\n- Member state: The Netherlands\n- Date issued: 20 May 2022\n- OIML issuer ID: NL1 (NMi Certin B.V., OIML Issuing Authority NL1)\n\nIssuing authority:\n- name: NMi Certin B.V.\n- address: Thijsseweg 11, 2629 JA Delft, The Netherlands\n- person_responsible: M.Ph.D. Schmidt\n- phone: +31 88 636 2332\n- email: certin@nmi.nl\n- website: www.nmi.nl\n- oiml_issuer_id: NL1\n\nApplicants:\n- ABB Limited, Oldends Lane, GL10 3TA Stonehouse, United Kingdom\n\nManufacturers: same (Applicant and Manufacturer: ABB Limited)\n\nCertified type:\n- category: Water meter (the schema says \"Gas meter\" etc. — \"Water meter\" fits)\n- type_designations: \"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z\", \"FET4 *\" — Actually the type is \"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z and FET4 *\". So type designations: [\"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z\", \"AquaMaster4 FET4*\"] — hmm. The pattern: FEW4XY.Z and FET4*. With X being 1 or 3, Y being 1, 2, 8 or 9, Z being R, V or F. Let me include type_designations: [\"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z\", \"AquaMaster4 FET4*\"] or maybe just as given: \"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z and FET4 *\". I'll list [\"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z\", \"AquaMaster4 FET4 *\"].\n\nRecommendation:\n- id: R49 (canonical). Actually \"R 49-1 (2013)\" — this is R 49-1. Canonical id probably \"R49\". Edition? The recommendation is R 49-1 (2013). Hmm, R49 has parts 1-3. The id should be \"R49\", edition... The 2013 edition. But part 1. The schema says id canonical e.g. \"R60\". I'll use \"R49-1\"? The rule says canonical e.g. \"R60\" not \"OIML R 60\". Hmm. R49-1 is part 1 of R49. I think \"R49-1\" is reasonable, but the example suggests stripping to the base recommendation. R 49-1 is technically a distinct document within OIML R 49. I'll use \"R49\" with edition 2013 and maybe note part... Actually the schema has no \"part\" field. Let me think: \"R 49-1 (2013)\" — the edition is 2013. I could set id \"R49\", edition 2013, and accuracy_classes [1, 2]. Alternatively id \"R49-1\". Given canonicalization, \"R49\" seems safer with edition 2013. Hmm, but losing \"-1\" loses information. There's no field for part. I'll use \"R49-1\" as the id since that's the actual document designation... The instruction: id (canonical, e.g. \"R60\" not \"OIML R 60\"). The canonical id of this recommendation document is R 49-1. I'll go with \"R49-1\". Actually, let me reconsider: many extraction pipelines index by \"R49\". The example given converts \"OIML R 60\" → \"R60\", removing prefix and spaces. Applying same: \"R 49-1\" → \"R49-1\". I'll use \"R49-1\".\n\n- edition: 2013\n- scheme: A\n- accuracy_classes: [1, 2]\n\nTest reports:\n- NMi-2254799-01, 14 August 2020, 235 pages, role: type_evaluation_report (these are \"results of tests and examinations provided in the associated reports\" — likely type evaluation reports). The documentation folder is no. T11851-5 — role: documentation_file.\n\nReports:\n1. NMi-2254799-01, 2020-08-14, 235 pages\n2. NMi-2254799-03, 2020-09-30, 44 pages\n3. NMi-2254799-04, 2020-12-24, 180 pages\n4. NMi-2564429-01, 2021-08-02, 26 pages\n5. NMi-2496280-01, 2021-08-26, 29 pages\n6. NMi-2496280-02, 2021-11-23, 25 pages\n\nDocumentation folder T11851-5 — test_reports[] with role documentation_file, pages unknown → omit pages? The schema says test_reports[]: id, date, pages, role. Documentation folder — I'll include with role \"documentation_file\", no date.\n\nType-level characteristics (Table 1):\n- measuring_principle: \"Electromagnetic\"\n- accuracy_class: \"1 and 2\" — but also in recommendation. Could be type_level value string \"1 and 2\"? Values must be number/range/string. \"1 and 2\" → maybe list? Rules say lists of values are model matrices, but accuracy class isn't a model matrix. I could represent as string \"1 and 2\". Hmm. Better: value: \"1 and 2\"? Or omit since it's in recommendation. I'll include as string.\n- environmental_class: \"O (installed outdoors)\" → value \"O\", maybe note. I'll do value: \"O\" with a separate? Keep simple: value \"O\", and add attribute environmental_class_location? Simpler: value: \"O (installed outdoors)\". Hmm, structured values strict — string value is allowed. I'll use value \"O\" and add... I'll just use value \"O (installed outdoors)\"? That mixes. Let me do value: \"O\" and mention installed outdoors in description? There's no place. I'll do value: \"O (installed outdoors)\" as string — acceptable.\n\nActually cleaner: measuring_principle, environmental_class: {value: \"O\"}, environmental_class_note: \"installed outdoors\"? Too fussy. String value fine.\n\n- electromagnetic_environment: \"E2\"\n- mechanical_class: \"M1\"\n- temperature_range_ambient: {min: -25, max: 55}, unit °C, note \"for both flow sensor and flow transmitter\" — could add footnote-like. I'll include as part of... the schema has footnote_markers only. I'll add a note in the value? No. I could add config-level? It applies to whole type. I'll keep value range and maybe include the note as attribute \"temperature_range_ambient_scope\". Simpler: just the range.\n- water_temperature_class: \"T50\" with range +0.1 to +50 °C. Split: water_temperature_class: \"T50\", water_temperature_range: {min: 0.1, max: 50}, unit °C. Actually \"+0,1 °C / +50 °C\" → {min: 0.1, max: 50}.\n- maximum_admissible_pressure: 1.6 MPa; also 16 bar. Primary: value 1.6, unit \"MPa\"; could add maximum_admissible_pressure_bar: 16 bar. I'll add both? Maybe one with unit MPa and footnote... I'll include map: value 1.6 MPa, and a second characteristic max_admissible_pressure_bar: 16 bar. Hmm, could be noisy. I'll include both since both explicit.\n- orientation: \"All positions (horizontal, vertical, or diagonal)\" — string.\n- flow_profile_sensitivity_class: complicated: \"U0 and D0 (0 x DN upstream and 0 x DN downstream), except for full bore sizes DN250 and larger which have: U3 and D0\". This varies by configuration (bore size ≥DN250 vs smaller). Could be config_level with axis \"bore_size\". Let me do:\n  config_level: [{attribute: \"flow_profile_sensitivity_class\", axis: \"meter_size\", values: [{condition: \"full bore DN250 and larger\", value: \"U3 and D0 (3 x DN upstream, 0 x DN downstream)\"}, {condition: \"all other sizes\", value: \"U0 and D0 (0 x DN upstream, 0 x DN downstream)\"}]}]\n\n- reverse_flow: \"The sensor is intended to measure reverse flow\" → boolean-ish string. value: true? I'll use value: \"intended\" hmm. Maybe attribute reverse_flow_measurement: {value: true}. I'll do that.\n\n- pressure_loss_class: varies by bore size and bore type:\n  Reduced bore: DN40 & DN50: Δp40 (0.40 bar); >DN50: Δp63 (0.63 bar)\n  (Virtual) full bore: DN40 & DN50: Δp10 (0.10 bar); DN65–DN200: Δp16 (0.16 bar); DN250+: Δp10 (0.10 bar)\n  → config_level with attribute \"pressure_loss_class\", axis \"bore_type_and_size\". Values:\n  - {condition: \"reduced bore, DN40-DN50\", value: \"Δp 40\", unit... } The class Δp40 corresponds to 0.40 bar. Value structured: value \"Δp 40\"? Or value 0.40 bar. I'll record pressure loss as numeric bar values with class in... hmm.\n\n  Maybe simpler: attribute \"pressure_loss\", axis: \"bore type / size\", values: [{condition: \"reduced bore, DN40–DN50\", value: 0.40, unit_symbol: \"bar\", footnote? }, {condition: \"reduced bore, larger than DN50\", value: 0.63, unit \"bar\"}, {condition: \"(virtual) full bore, DN40–DN50\", value: 0.10}, {condition: \"(virtual) full bore, DN65–DN200\", value: 0.16}, {condition: \"(virtual) full bore, DN250 and larger\", value: 0.10}]. And class labels: value could be object? Schema value: number | {min,max} | string. I could use string \"Δp 40 (0.40 bar)\". That keeps both. But rule 2 wants numbers with units separated. I'll do two attributes? Getting heavy. I'll use value: \"Δp 40\" plus... hmm.\n\n  Compromise: config_level entries with attribute \"pressure_loss_class\" (string values like \"Δp 40\") and attribute \"maximum_pressure_loss\" (numeric bar). That doubles entries but is clean. Actually I'll just do one attribute \"pressure_loss\" with value string \"Δp 40 (0.40 bar)\"? The strictness rule is about numeric values; class labels are fine as strings. But then the bar number is embedded.\n\n  I'll do: attribute \"pressure_loss_class\", values: [{condition: ..., value: \"Δp 40 (0.40 bar)\"}...] — one config_level set. Acceptable.\n\n  Hmm, actually let me be more structured: value: {min: 0.40, max: 0.40}? No, that's wrong semantics. Class is a designation. Keep string.\n\n- power supply:\n  - AC mains: 85–240 VAC @ 50/60 Hz → power_supply_voltage_ac: {min: 85, max: 240} V; power_supply_frequency: {min: 50, max: 60} Hz; power_supply_type: \"AC\"\n  - DC: 6–32 V → power_supply_voltage_dc: {min: 6, max: 32} V (renewable energy power)\n  - Battery: 2x 3.6 V, operating range 2.9–3.6 V → battery_voltage_nominal: 3.6 V; battery_quantity: 2; battery_operating_range: {min: 2.9, max: 3.6} V\n\n- software: version numbers with checksums — software versions. Could be type_level strings: software_identification: [\"03.00.03 (checksum B26AFE6B)\", ...]. The schema characteristic value can be string. Multiple versions — maybe list as separate entries: software_version attributes. I'll add three entries under type_level? Keys must be unique. I'll do software_identification: {value: \"03.00.03 (checksum B26AFE6B); 03.00.04 (checksum 6E98963A); 03.02.00 (checksum E12E5C04)\"} as one string. Or better: a small matrix? It's not model-level. I'll do one string value.\n\nActually, could do:\n\"software_version\": [{value: \"03.00.03\", checksum...}] — no per-value metadata field. I'll just do the combined string. Or three characteristics: software_version_1 etc. — ugly. Combined string it is.\n\n- checking_facilities: \"Type P (permanent) automatic checking facility\" → string value \"Type P (permanent) automatic\".\n\nMatrix table (Table 2): Reduced bore - AC mains powered. Columns: meter_size (DN), accuracy_class, q1 (min), q2 (transitional), q3 (permanent), q4 (overload), ratio_q3_q1.\n\nRows (converting commas):\n- DN40, class 1: 0.05, 0.08, 25, 31.25, 500\n- DN40, class 2: 0.025, 0.04, 25, 31.25, 1000\n- DN50, class 1: 0.08, 0.128, 40, 50, 500\n- DN50, class 2: 0.04, 0.064, 40, 50, 1000\n- DN65, 1&2: 0.063, 0.1008, 63, 78.75, 1000\n- DN80, 1&2: 0.1, 0.16, 100, 125, 1000\n- DN100, 1: 0.32, 0.512, 160, 200, 500\n- DN100, 2: 0.16, 0.256, 160, 200, 1000\n- DN125, 1: 0.32, 0.512, 160, 200, 500\n- DN125, 2: 0.16, 0.256, 160, 200, 1000\n- DN150, 1&2: 0.4, 0.64, 400, 500, 1000\n- DN200, 1&2: 0.63, 1.008, 630, 787.5, 1000\n- DN250, 1: 2, 3.2, 1000, 1250, 500\n- DN250, 2: 1, 1.6, 1000, 1250, 1000\n- DN300, 1: 3.2, 5.12, 1600, 2000, 500\n- DN300, 2: 1.6, 2.56, 1600, 2000, 1000\n- DN350, 1: 3.2, 5.12, 1600, 2000, 500\n- DN350, 2: 1.6, 2.56, 1600, 2000, 1000\n- DN400, 1&2: 5, 8, 2500, 3125, 500\n- DN450, 1&2: 5, 8, 2500, 3125, 500\n- DN500, 1: 8, 12.8, 4000, 5000, 500\n- DN500, 2: 4, 6.4, 4000, 5000, 1000\n- DN600, 1: 12.6, 20.16, 6300, 7875, 500\n- DN600, 2: 6.3, 10.08, 6300, 7875, 1000\n\nNote: DN600 class 1 q1 is \"12,6\" — suspicious (pattern suggests 12.6? Actually Q3=6300, ratio 500 → Q1 = 6300/500 = 12.6. Yes 12.6. And class 2: 6300/1000 = 6.3. Consistent.)\n\nModel family: The cert covers a family of instruments (Table 2 explicitly says \"family of instruments\"). Models are meter sizes × accuracy classes? The model variants here are sizes DN40–DN600 with classes. Per the schema, model_family.models: one entry per variant. I'll create model_id like \"DN40-class 1\"? Hmm. But the table's rows combine size and class. The actual product variants are sizes; class 1&2 rows share values. I could create models per size: DN40, DN50, ..., DN600. But then q1 varies by class within a size → that becomes model_level with values differing per... no, model_level maps attribute → values per model; if within one model there are two class-dependent values, that breaks the shape.\n\nOptions:\na) models keyed by size+class (e.g., \"DN40/class 1\") — 24 models, matrix rows map directly.\nb) models per size (16 sizes... actually 16 sizes: 40,50,65,80,100,125,150,200,250,300,350,400,450,500,600 = 15 sizes). Then flow rates per class become config_level with axis \"accuracy_class\".\n\nI think (a) is cleaner for model_level extraction but produces model ids like \"DN40-class1\". Alternatively keep matrix_tables for the full table and populate model_level for attributes that are constant per size: q3, q4 constant per size. q1, q2, ratio vary by class.\n\nThe R137 example: table rows per size, each row one model. Here rows per size×class. Given the fragment covers only Table 2 reduced bore AC mains (page 3 shows only this table; there might be more tables on later pages for full bore, battery powered etc., not in this fragment), I'll:\n\n- model_family.models: one per size+class combo? Or per size?\n\nHmm. Since Table 2's caption is \"Reduced bore - AC mains powered\", this is one config of the family. The models are sizes; class is an attribute axis. I'll make models per size: [\"DN40\", \"DN50\", \"DN65\", \"DN80\", \"DN100\", \"DN125\", \"DN150\", \"DN200\", \"DN250\", \"DN300\", \"DN350\", \"DN400\", \"DN450\", \"DN500\", \"DN600\"].\n\nThen model_level:\n- q3 permanent flow rate: values per size (m³/h)\n- q4 overload flow rate: per size\n\nAnd config_level (axis \"accuracy_class\"):\n- q1 minimum flow rate: {condition: \"DN40, class 1\", value: 0.05}... that's a 2D matrix (size × class) which config_level's shape (attribute, axis, values[{condition, value}]) can hold with condition strings \"DN40 accuracy class 1\".\n\nThat's verbose but correct. Alternatively just rely on matrix_tables for the full data and include a modest model_level for q3/q4 plus config_level for q1/q2/ratio. That's a lot of JSON but it's the \"MOST IMPORTANT\" rule. Let me do it.\n\nActually, maybe simpler and defensible: treat each (size, class) as a model variant, since q1/q2/ratio differ. Model ids \"DN40/class 1\" etc. Then model_level attributes: q1, q2, q3, q4, ratio_q3_q1 — five attributes × 24 models. That's 120 value entries. Versus approach b: model_level q3, q4 (15×2=30 entries) + config_level q1, q2, ratio (24×3=72 condition entries). Similar size.\n\nI'll go with approach (a): models keyed \"DN40/class 1\"... Hmm, but is class part of model identity? In water meters, a meter size is certified for both classes with different Q3/Q1 ratio — the ratio selection is a configuration of the same device. Approach (b) is more faithful.\n\nLet me go with (b):\n- model_family.models: 15 sizes.\n- model_level: q3, q4 per size.\n- config_level: axis \"accuracy_class\" for q1, q2, ratio with conditions \"DN40 accuracy class 1\" etc.\n\nActually wait — maybe better: axis could be \"size and accuracy class\". Fine.\n\nConfig entries:\n\nq1 (m³/h):\n- DN40: class1 0.05, class2 0.025\n- DN50: class1 0.08, class2 0.04\n- DN65: 1&2: 0.063\n- DN80: 1&2: 0.1\n- DN100: c1 0.32, c2 0.16\n- DN125: c1 0.32, c2 0.16\n- DN150: 1&2: 0.4\n- DN200: 1&2: 0.63\n- DN250: c1 2, c2 1\n- DN300: c1 3.2, c2 1.6\n- DN350: c1 3.2, c2 1.6\n- DN400: 1&2: 5\n- DN450: 1&2: 5\n- DN500: c1 8, c2 4\n- DN600: c1 12.6, c2 6.3\n\nq2:\n- DN40: 0.08 / 0.04\n- DN50: 0.128 / 0.064\n- DN65: 0.1008\n- DN80: 0.16\n- DN100: 0.512 / 0.256\n- DN125: 0.512 / 0.256\n- DN150: 0.64\n- DN200: 1.008\n- DN250: 3.2 / 1.6\n- DN300: 5.12 / 2.56\n- DN350: 5.12 / 2.56\n- DN400: 8\n- DN450: 8\n- DN500: 12.8 / 6.4\n- DN600: 20.16 / 10.08\n\nratio q3/q1:\n- DN40: 500/1000\n- DN50: 500/1000\n- DN65: 1000\n- DN80: 1000\n- DN100: 500/1000\n- DN125: 500/1000\n- DN150: 1000\n- DN200: 1000\n- DN250: 500/1000\n- DN300: 500/1000\n- DN350: 500/1000\n- DN400: 500\n- DN450: 500\n- DN500: 500/1000\n- DN600: 500/1000\n\nq3 per size: 25, 40, 63, 100, 160, 160, 400, 630, 1000, 1600, 1600, 2500, 2500, 4000, 6300\nq4: 31.25, 50, 78.75, 125, 200, 200, 500, 787.5, 1250, 2000, 2000, 3125, 3125, 5000, 7875\n\nmatrix_tables: include the full table with name \"Reduced bore – AC mains powered\", columns, column_units, rows (24 rows).\n\nGiven the schema example includes \"column_units\", I'll include it.\n\nCertificate-level:\n- number: \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\" (include revision separately? schema: number, scheme, project_number, page_total, member_state, date_issued, oiml_issuer_id). Revision 6 — there's no revision field at certificate level; revision_history is for revisions of the certificate. The fragment says \"revision 6\" but no revision history details. I could put number \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\" and note revision in... maybe number: \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01 revision 6\"? Better keep number clean: \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\". Add revision_history? We only know current revision 6 without dates/changes — rule 8 says don't fabricate; I could add {revision: \"6\"} without date/changes? The schema requires date and changes; omit since not present. Hmm, but the number includes \"revision 6\". I'll include certificate.number as \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\" and... Actually there's no field for current revision except revision_history. I'll skip revision_history since no dates/changes given.\n\n- scheme: \"A\"\n- project_number: \"3489487\"\n- page_total: 11\n- member_state: \"The Netherlands\"\n- date_issued: \"2022-05-20\"\n- oiml_issuer_id: \"NL1\"\n\nIssuing authority:\n- name: \"NMi Certin B.V.\"\n- address_lines: [\"Thijsseweg 11\", \"2629 JA Delft\", \"The Netherlands\"]\n- person_responsible: \"M.Ph.D. Schmidt\" (from page 1)\n- phone: \"+31 88 636 2332\"\n- email: \"certin@nmi.nl\"\n- website: \"www.nmi.nl\"\n- oiml_issuer_id: \"NL1\"\n\nCertified type:\n- category: \"Water meter\"\n- type_designations: [\"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z\", \"AquaMaster4 FET4*\"]. Original: \"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z and FET4 *\". The * pattern applies to both presumably. I'll list [\"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z\", \"AquaMaster4 FET4*\"]. Hmm, maybe keep exactly: \"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z\" and \"FET4 *\". The full type name is AquaMaster4; FEW and FET are sensor series (FEW = flow electromagnetic water? FET?). I'll write type_designations: [\"AquaMaster4 FEW4XY.Z\", \"AquaMaster4 FET4*\"].\n- module_designation: null? Not applicable — omit or null. I'll omit (rule 8) or set null. Schema shows it as field; \"Not applicable\" for R60. I'll set null... Rule 8: omit if not present. I'll omit.\n- description: \"An electromagnetic water meter\" plus variant explanation: X ∈ {1,3}, Y ∈ {1,2,8,9}, Z ∈ {R,V,F}. I'll put description capturing that.\n\nmodel_family.family_name: \"AquaMaster4\" — explicit family name. Yes.\n\ncomponents: The cert mentions \"flow sensor and flow transmitter\" (temperature range note). Types FEW (flow sensor?) and FET (transmitter?). Actually FEW4 is the sensor, FET4 the transmitter (remote version). Hmm: \"Y being 1, 2, 8 or 9 (integral/remote version, optionally sensor only)\". FEW = electromagnetic flow sensor; FET = ... Actually AquaMaster4: FEW4 is flanged sensor; FET... I shouldn't fabricate roles beyond what's stated. The cert says \"for both flow sensor and flow transmitter\". I could add components: flow sensor (FEW4XY.Z) and flow transmitter (FET4*)? That's inference. The type designations FEW4XY.Z and FET4* — the \"and\" suggests two variants rather than components. Given uncertainty, I'll omit components[] to avoid fabrication. Hmm, rule 6 says include only if cert names sub-assemblies. The temperature note names flow sensor and flow transmitter as parts. But type designations per component are not explicitly assigned (FEW vs FET mapping is inferred). I'll include components with roles \"flow sensor\" (FEW4XY.Z) and \"flow transmitter\" (FET4*)? Risky. Actually, in ABB AquaMaster3/4 documentation: \"AquaMaster FEW\" = sensor, \"FET\" = transmitter for remote... I believe FET4 is indeed the remote transmitter variant. The note \"optionally sensor only\" for Y suggests some variants are sensor-only. I'll omit components to be safe — the fragment doesn't clearly name sub-assemblies with designations.\n\nCharacteristics type_level — also accuracy_class \"1 and 2\" appears both in table and recommendation. I'll include in type_level as string \"1 and 2\".\n\nLet me also consider \"environmental class O (installed outdoors)\": I'll put value \"O\" and a separate note? I'll just use value \"O (installed outdoors)\".\n\nHmm, let me reconsider strictness: string values are allowed for non-numeric. Fine.\n\nWater temperature: T50 class with range 0.1–50 °C. \"+0,1 °C / +50 °C\" → {min: 0.1, max: 50}.\n\nMAP: 1.6 MPa (16 bar): value 1.6, unit \"MPa\"; plus second attribute maximum_admissible_pressure_bar value 16 unit \"bar\"? I'll include just MPa and add \"maximum_admissible_pressure_bar\": 16 \"bar\". Both are legit. I'll include both.\n\nPower supply:\n- power_supply_voltage_ac: {min: 85, max: 240}, unit \"V\"\n- power_supply_frequency: {min: 50, max: 60}, unit \"Hz\"\n- power_supply_voltage_dc: {min: 6, max: 32}, unit \"V\"\n- battery_quantity: 2\n- battery_cell_voltage_nominal: 3.6, unit \"V\"\n- battery_operating_voltage_range: {min: 2.9, max: 3.6}, unit \"V\"\n\nReverse flow: attribute \"reverse_flow_measurement\": {value: \"intended (sensor measures reverse flow)\"}? I'll do value true? I'll use value: \"The sensor is intended to measure reverse flow\" — too verbose. Simpler: reverse_flow_supported: true. I'll do that.\n\nFlow profile sensitivity: config_level:\naxis: \"bore size (full bore)\"\nvalues: [{condition: \"full bore sizes DN250 and larger\", value: \"U3 and D0 (3 x DN upstream, 0 x DN downstream)\"}, {condition: \"all other sizes\", value: \"U0 and D0 (0 x DN upstream, 0 x DN downstream)\"}]\n\nPressure loss: config_level:\naxis: \"bore type and size\"\nvalues:\n- {condition: \"reduced bore DN40–DN50\", value: \"Δp 40 (0.40 bar)\"}\n- {condition: \"reduced bore larger than DN50\", value: \"Δp 63 (0.63 bar)\"}\n- {condition: \"(virtual) full bore DN40–DN50\", value: \"Δp 10 (0.10 bar)\"}\n- {condition: \"(virtual) full bore DN65–DN200\", value: \"Δp 16 (0.16 bar)\"}\n- {condition: \"(virtual) full bore DN250 and larger\", value: \"Δp 10 (0.10 bar)\"}\n\nAlternatively split class and max pressure loss. I'll also add numeric max pressure loss attribute? To reduce bloat, keep combined strings. Hmm, the strict-value rule says numbers should be structured... but here value is fundamentally a class designation with a parenthetical bar value. I'll split into two config_level sets: pressure_loss_class (strings) and maximum_pressure_loss (bar numbers). That's more schema-conformant. Let me do both — it's only 5 entries each.\n\nActually, to keep the output manageable, I'll do just the numeric \"maximum_pressure_loss\" with class embedded in condition? No — class is the data point. I'll do both sets. Fine.\n\nSoftware: I'll make it a matrix? \"software_identification\" — I'll do type_level with a string. Or better: three separate... I'll do:\n\ntype_level:\n\"software_versions\": {value: \"03.00.03 (checksum B26AFE6B), 03.00.04 (checksum 6E98963A), 03.02.00 (checksum E12E5C04)\"}\n\nHmm, maybe cleaner as matrix_tables? No. String is fine. Actually, could I do software_version entries with checksums as separate attributes?\n- software_version_1: \"03.00.03\", checksum_1: \"B26AFE6B\"... ugly.\nOne string it is.\n\nChecking facilities: \"Type P (permanent) automatic checking facility\" → checking_facility: {value: \"Type P (permanent) automatic\"}.\n\nMeasuring principle: \"Electromagnetic\".\n\nOrientation: \"All positions (horizontal, vertical, or diagonal)\".\n\nNow the certificate date: \"20 May 2022\" appears near \"Issuing Authority: NMi Certin B.V., OIML Issuing Authority NL1 / Certification Board\". That's the issue date. → \"2022-05-20\".\n\nAccuracy classes for recommendation: [1, 2].\n\nNow assemble matrix_tables:\n\n{\n  \"name\": \"Table 2 – General characteristics of the family of instruments (reduced bore, 